Split a boundary edge where the face's normal turns more than an interior
link may.

What it fixes
  BRepMesh_CurveTessellator discretises an edge by its own curve — chord
  and tangent deflection along the curve, GCPnts — and, per face, splits a
  segment whose surface point at the pcurve's middle strays from the chord.
  How the face's normal turns along the edge is never asked. A straight
  ruling of a twisted wall (a bilinear patch through four corners, or a
  ruled loft between two sections turned against each other) is one link
  however far the normal turns along it, though every interior link is held
  to AngleInterior (BRepMesh_DelaunayDeflectionControlMeshAlgo,
  checkLinkEndsForAngularDeviation, default 2 * Angle = 1 rad).

  The interior then chases what only the boundary can give. The triangle
  standing on the ruling has an apex, the apex's link to the ruling's far
  corner spans the whole turn, the angular check splits that link at its
  parametric middle, and the new node is the next apex, half as far from
  the ruling and joined to the far corner by a link just as long. Each
  pass halves the distance; only the pass cap ends it, at d / 2^11. On the
  two walls sharing a ruling of parcad's untriangle-v3 the chains ended
  0.0008 mm from the ruling on x86_64, and parcad welds mesh vertices at
  0.001 mm, so the two faces' last nodes became one vertex with four
  triangles on each of its edges and the part was refused as not
  watertight; arm64's rounding stopped the chain a pass short, and the
  same part built there.

  splitSegment now also splits a segment when the surface normals at its
  ends turn by more than AngleInterior, with the GeomLib::NormEstim the
  interior check uses. The node lands on the edge, shared by every face
  that has the edge, so the two walls agree on it by construction; and
  with nodes along the ruling no more than AngleInterior apart, a Delaunay
  triangulation cannot hold a triangle from an apex near the ruling to a
  far ruling node — its circumcircle would swallow the nodes between — so
  the apex joins its neighbours on the ruling, whose normals it is within
  AngleInterior of, and the chain never starts.

  A straight edge whose face is a plane, cylinder or cone turns no normal
  along it and is left as it was; a curved edge is already split by its
  own tangent deflection at Angle, half of AngleInterior, before this test
  sees it. What changes is the straight edge of a twisted or a ruled face.

How it was measured
  parcad's eval corpus, 193 cases, built in a Linux container under GCC
  13 on x86_64 and on arm64, every recorded volume, size, face and edge
  count held to its tolerance; and crates/parcad-occt/examples/
  mesh_deviation, every triangle sampled at 15 barycentric points and each
  projected onto its face's surface, the worst distance and the count of
  samples over the 0.01 mm the mesher was asked for.
  - untriangle-v3 on x86_64: refused, 2 of 38616 mesh edges bordering one
    face -> builds watertight, 12288 triangles, 24801.75 mm3, worst sample
    0.0100 mm and none over; on arm64 12726 -> 12288 triangles, 0.0096 ->
    0.0100 mm worst, none over either way. Its bed contact, which the
    case's own note says is read off saddle-wall slivers, reports two
    patches instead of one on both builds; the case records that.
  - twisted-loft, the corpus's other ruled twist: 3860 -> 3772 triangles
    on arm64 and 3892 -> 3804 on x86_64, worst 0.0099 -> 0.0100 mm, none
    over; volume 1999.93 -> 2000.39 mm3 against 2000 exact. Recorded.
  - Two other cases moved, both within tolerance and both pleated walls:
    pleated-shade-box-cut 136640 -> 136636 triangles (arm64), worst
    0.0244 mm before and after, 549 -> 501 samples over; pleated-shade
    3492540 -> 3492416, worst 0.0789 mm before this patch, 9759 samples
    over: both were outside 0.01 mm already. Nothing else changed by one
    triangle: 7996503 -> 7995849 over the arm64 run, the involute gear at
    1996 as patch 0003 left it.
  - The parts a boundary split could touch, measured before -> after:
    helical-groove 0.0088 -> 0.0088 mm worst, helical-spring 0.0120 ->
    0.0120 (508 over, both), left-hand-hook 0.0108 -> 0.0108 (4 over,
    both); none of their counts moved by more than 1 %.
  - pipe-tee is refused on x86_64 before and after, by the exact-geometry
    self-crossing check on its 2 mm blend; arm64 builds it both times.
    docs/NEXT.md carries it; it is not this patch's.
  A MinSize floor against frontier links in rejectByMinSize, and moving
  the inserted node to the parametric middle, were both built and
  measured first: the floor changed nothing (the chain runs through the
  angular check, which never asks it), and the node move left six of
  seven measured parts outside their deflection (untriangle-v3 0.0096 ->
  0.0166 mm). Neither shipped.

Upstream status
  Not yet offered upstream (github.com/Open-Cascade-SAS/OCCT).
